\@writefile{lof}{\contentsline {figure}{\numberline {2.1}{\ignorespaces The pipeline chain made concrete: real $|U|^2$ intensity at the corneal plane (left column, step~1 of the chain above, post-pinhole/pre-lens) versus the real retinal intensity after the full lens-phase and myopic-axial-distance propagation (right column, step~5). Top row $M=0$D, $a_{\mathrm  {hole}}=2000\,\mu $m; bottom row $M=8$D, $a_{\mathrm  {hole}}=400\,\mu $m -- the two extremes of the real per-$M$ aperture map (\texttt  {array\_data/evaluation\_results/} \texttt  {PRIMARY\_WINNER\_PER\_M.csv}, mtime 2026-09-13 14:10). The corneal-plane panels show genuine near-field Fresnel ringing at the aperture edge -- the field has propagated only $d_{\mathrm  {gap}}=12$\,mm and still closely resembles the aperture's own geometric shape; the retinal panels show the same energy after the myopic axial propagation, now dominated by defocus blur rather than the aperture's edge structure. Both column pairs generated by the same \texttt  {run\_pipeline\_point()} call (one call yields both fields; no extra propagation was run to make this comparison) -- \texttt  {figure\_scripts/build\_corneal\_illumination.py} (this paper, new) and \texttt  {Array\_Design/array\_geometry\_lab/retinal\_light\_distribution\_simulation/} \texttt  {realistic\_heatmaps/build\_realistic\_heatmaps.py} (mtime of its outputs: 2026-09-28 11:15--11:16). Each panel normalized to its own peak -- absolute brightness is not comparable across panels without a source-power calibration this project has not made (Section~\ref {sec:limitations-optics}).}}{16}{figure.2.1}\protected@file@percent }
\newlabel{fig:cornea-vs-retina}{{2.1}{16}{The pipeline chain made concrete: real $|U|^2$ intensity at the corneal plane (left column, step~1 of the chain above, post-pinhole/pre-lens) versus the real retinal intensity after the full lens-phase and myopic-axial-distance propagation (right column, step~5). Top row $M=0$D, $a_{\mathrm {hole}}=2000\,\mu $m; bottom row $M=8$D, $a_{\mathrm {hole}}=400\,\mu $m -- the two extremes of the real per-$M$ aperture map (\texttt {array\_data/evaluation\_results/} \texttt {PRIMARY\_WINNER\_PER\_M.csv}, mtime 2026-09-13 14:10). The corneal-plane panels show genuine near-field Fresnel ringing at the aperture edge -- the field has propagated only $d_{\mathrm {gap}}=12$\,mm and still closely resembles the aperture's own geometric shape; the retinal panels show the same energy after the myopic axial propagation, now dominated by defocus blur rather than the aperture's edge structure. Both column pairs generated by the same \texttt {run\_pipeline\_point()} call (one call yields both fields; no extra propagation was run to make this comparison) -- \texttt {figure\_scripts/build\_corneal\_illumination.py} (this paper, new) and \texttt {Array\_Design/array\_geometry\_lab/retinal\_light\_distribution\_simulation/} \texttt {realistic\_heatmaps/build\_realistic\_heatmaps.py} (mtime of its outputs: 2026-09-28 11:15--11:16). Each panel normalized to its own peak -- absolute brightness is not comparable across panels without a source-power calibration this project has not made (Section~\ref {sec:limitations-optics})}{figure.2.1}{}}
\citation{matsushima2009}
